MASTER STYLE:

Premium Korean webtoon artwork drawn with exceptionally refined
professional ink linework.

This is NOT a line-art conversion.
This is NOT a grayscale version of a colored illustration.

The image must look like an actual Korean webtoon artist originally drew
the scene directly with ink.

The uploaded character sheet is the ABSOLUTE CHARACTER DESIGN REFERENCE for
SHAPE, not for rendering style. Preserve the character's identity,
hairstyle, hair length, hair shape, facial structure, eye shape, body
proportions, clothing design, accessories, weapons, and distinctive
details from the character sheet.
Do not redesign the character.
Do not simplify the costume.
Do not invent unrelated accessories.
Do not copy the character sheet's own rendering, coloring, or finish — how
this panel is drawn is decided entirely by this MASTER STYLE, not by the
sheet.

The sheet's colors (hair color, eye color, outfit color) describe the
character's design ONLY. They are not an instruction to render the
character in full color. Actual color placement in the final image is
governed entirely by the COLOR rules below — most of the character must
still end up as black-and-white ink line art, with color appearing only at
the few selective accent points chosen for this scene. Rendering the
character's hair or outfit in their full sheet color throughout the panel
is treated the same as "coloring the entire character" and is wrong here.

Prioritize, in this order:
1. character drawing quality
2. facial expression
3. anatomy
4. hair rendering
5. clothing construction
6. important props
7. cinematic composition

Line density must be controlled by visual importance, not applied evenly.

Characters and important foreground objects: highly refined, intricate
linework. Secondary background: simpler and lighter linework.

Do NOT fill every surface with unnecessary hatching.
Do NOT make the entire image equally detailed.
Do NOT turn architecture into technical drawings.

Use a line-weight hierarchy: heavy confident contours, medium structural
lines, fine delicate detail lines.

Use selective hatching only where it improves form, depth, lighting, or
material — not as a uniform texture laid over everything.

The image should have clear areas of visual rest.

The result should feel like a skilled webtoon artist spent a long time
drawing ONE finished panel, not like an AI generated an extremely detailed
coloring page.

COLOR — BLACK AND WHITE DOMINANT:

Only 1-3 small areas may contain vivid color accents. Examples: red eyes,
blood, a few flower petals, a magical glow, one gemstone, a small portion
of clothing. Everything else remains monochrome.

Color accents must be isolated and intentional, like an artistic emphasis
placed on an otherwise black-and-white webtoon panel — not a wash or
gradient, and never the character's full hair, full outfit, or the whole
background.

DO NOT color the entire character.
DO NOT color the entire background.
DO NOT use soft full-image gradients.
DO NOT turn the image into a full-color anime illustration.

CHARACTER EMPHASIS:

For emotional scenes, prioritize facial expression and eyes. For action
scenes, prioritize anatomy, gesture, foreshortening, motion, hair movement,
clothing movement, weapon trajectory, impact effects, and dynamic
perspective. For quiet scenes, prioritize atmosphere, composition,
environmental details, subtle expressions, and controlled line density.

BACKGROUND:

Background exists to place the character, not to compete with them. Use
simpler, lighter linework than the character — a few confident lines
establishing the space, not an equally-detailed drawing. Do not turn
buildings or architecture into technical line drawings.

COMPOSITION:

Treat the image as an actual webtoon panel, not a character sheet. Use an
intentional camera angle and strong composition. Avoid static centered
character posing unless explicitly requested. The composition should
communicate the scene and emotion immediately.

IMPORTANT:

The uploaded character sheet determines WHAT the character looks like. The
scene description determines WHAT the character is doing. This MASTER
STYLE determines HOW the scene is drawn — including the linework, the
density, and the color placement.

Never sacrifice character consistency for artistic variation.
Never replace the character with a generic anime character.
Never convert the image into a full-color illustration.

Final visual target: one exceptionally well-drawn Korean webtoon panel,
predominantly black-and-white ink, with controlled line density (detailed
character, lighter background) and only a few carefully chosen vivid color
accents.

